+### Controlling Inlining
+
+Inlining is optimizer-controlled by default. To prevent a particular
+`NautilusFunction` from being inlined by either the MLIR or LLVM optimizer, set
+its `noinline` attribute before it is first traced:
+
+```cpp
+static auto nautilusAdd = NautilusFunction{"add", add};
+
+void configureFunctions() {
+    nautilusAdd.setNoInline();
+}
+```
+
+Calling `setNoInline(false)` restores the default optimizer-controlled behavior.
+The generic `setAttribute(key, value)` method is also available for backend
+attributes; `setNoInline()` is the type-safe convenience method for the
+supported `noinline` attribute.
+
 Call it like a regular function inside traced code:
 
 ```cpp
diff --git a/nautilus/include/nautilus/nautilus_function.hpp b/nautilus/include/nautilus/nautilus_function.hpp
index 309ee06..b06d26a 100644
--- a/nautilus/include/nautilus/nautilus_function.hpp
+++ b/nautilus/include/nautilus/nautilus_function.hpp
@@ -152,6 +152,19 @@ public:
 		return definition_.name();
 	}
 
+	/// Adds a backend attribute to this compiled function.
+	/// Attributes must be configured before the function is first traced.
+	NautilusFunction& setAttribute(std::string key, std::string value) {
+		definition_.setAttribute(key, value);
+		return *this;
+	}
+
+	/// Controls whether the MLIR and LLVM optimizers may inline this function.
+	/// Passing false restores the default optimizer-controlled behavior.
+	NautilusFunction& setNoInline(bool noInline = true) {
+		return setAttribute("noinline", noInline ? "true" : "false");
+	}
+
 	/// Returns a val wrapping the compiled function pointer for this NautilusFunction.
 	/// During tracing: emits a FUNC_ADDR operation that registers the function for compilation.
 	/// Outside tracing: JIT-compiles the function on demand and returns the compiled pointer.
